{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# RCA — CONFIRMED root cause (Phase 1 complete, hypothesis-driven, replicated)\n\n## Verdict in one line\n\n**Gerrit never fails.** All 14 slow `git-upload-pack` requests in the retained log returned\n**HTTP 200**. The 504 is manufactured entirely at the edge: nginx's Gerrit `location /` has\n**no `proxy_read_timeout`**, so it runs on the compiled-in **60 s** default, while a legitimate\nCI change-ref fetch on this host currently takes a **median 36 s / p90 92 s / max 144 s**.\nThe 60 s ceiling sits *inside* the normal duration distribution, so ~1/3 of CI fetches are\nkilled mid-flight and reported to the client as a server error.\n\n## The proximate defect (the changeable artifact)\n\n`infra/nginx/rebar.conf.template` — the Gerrit catch-all block:\n\n```nginx\nlocation / {\n    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;      # <- no proxy_read_timeout => nginx default 60s\n```\n\nEvery **other** proxied location in the same file sets one explicitly\n(`proxy_read_timeout 3600s;` at lines 207 / 223 / 246, the three `/mcp` blocks). The location\nserving the *longest* operations on the box — git pack generation — is the only one left on the\ndefault. Confirmed on the live host: `grep proxy_read_timeout /etc/nginx/conf.d/rebar.conf`\nreturns exactly three hits, all `3600s`, all `/mcp`.\n\n## Proof\n\n**1. The timeout is nginx's, and it is exactly 60 s.**\nCI run 33995047786: fetch issued `22:08:58.46`, 504 returned `22:09:58.73` = **60.27 s**.\nTicket `1e24-bfc0` independently recorded `duration_ms=61003`. My replication measured 60.8 s.\n\n**2. Every timed-out request SUCCEEDED at Gerrit.** From `/var/gerrit/logs/httpd_log`, the four\nincident fetches:\n\n| client | start | Gerrit result | Gerrit duration | nginx |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 145.132.103.177 | 22:08:58.72 | **200**, 131070 B | **85.3 s** | 504 at 60 s |\n| 172.182.195.43 | 22:08:52.22 | **200**, 131070 B | **91.8 s** | 504 at 60 s |\n| 64.236.160.194 | 15:39:25.22 | **200**, 98318 B | **92.4 s** | 504 at 60 s |\n| 20.168.98.41 | 15:39:33.41 | **200**, 98318 B | **93.6 s** | 504 at 60 s |\n\nAcross the whole retained log: **353 upload-pack POSTs; 14 exceeded 60 s; all 14 returned 200.**\nThe nginx access log records those same responses as `504 167` — 167 bytes is nginx's own error\npage. nginx discarded a successful upstream response and substituted an error.\n\n**3. Rate, predicted then replicated.** Anonymous (CI) substantive fetches: n=37, median 36.0 s,\np90 92.4 s, max 143.9 s, **12/37 = 32.4 % over 60 s**. Authenticated review-bot fetches are much\ncheaper (median 12.1 s, 1.6 % over). I then ran the *exact* CI command\n(`git fetch https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/rebar refs/changes/84/2684/2`) six times from the\nhost:\n\n```\niter=1 rc=0   74.7s      iter=4 rc=0   88.7s\niter=2 rc=0   80.1s      iter=5 rc=128 60.8s  HTTP 504\niter=3 rc=0   85.0s      iter=6 rc=128 68.1s  HTTP 504\n```\n\n**2/6 = 33 % — matching the 32.4 % predicted from the log.** The bug is reproducible on demand.\n\n**4. Why it is intermittent — and why some fetches survive past 90 s.**\n`proxy_read_timeout` is a **gap** timeout, not a total. Iterations 1-4 exceeded 60 s *in total*\nand still succeeded, because Gerrit kept emitting bytes. A 504 needs one **silent phase**\n(negotiation + `preparePack` counting/delta-compression, which produces no output) to exceed 60 s\non its own. That phase's length is a draw from a distribution whose median sits near the ceiling,\nso the same fetch fails and then succeeds seconds later. This is also why all\n**78/78** nginx timeouts are `while reading response header from upstream` and **zero** are\nbody-phase: the failure can only occur before the first byte.\n\n## Why the wall time is high (the contributing capacity condition — quantified)\n\nThe host is a **burstable `t4g.large` (2 vCPU / 7.8 GiB)**. CloudWatch:\n\n- `CPUCreditBalance` fell steadily through 2026-09-04 and hit **0.0 at 12:38 UTC on 2026-09-05**,\n  and has stayed 0.0 for the following 10 hours.\n- `CPUSurplusCreditBalance` has climbed continuously since: **15.5 -> 316.7**.\n- `CPUUtilization` sustained **55-74 %** against a **30 % baseline** for t4g.large.\n\n**All four incident 504s (15:38, 15:39, 21:52, 22:08 UTC) occurred after the credit balance hit\nzero.** The earlier 02:51-03:08 UTC cluster occurred while it was collapsing (74.9 -> 31.6 -> 13.7).\nConsistent with this, upload-pack burns only ~24 % of its wall time on CPU (median across 37 CI\nfetches) — it is off-CPU ~76 % of the time; `vmstat` showed `steal` at 13 % and load average\n9-12 on 2 vCPU. A full clone needs ~20-28 s of CPU and takes 85-144 s of wall.\n\nMemory is also tight: `rebar-mcp` holds **4.45 GB RSS (55.6 % of RAM)** at 47 % CPU, squeezing the\npage cache to ~600 MB on a git server.\n\n## REFUTED — the ticket's own leading hypothesis\n\n**The gate-scratch / root-filesystem IO story is NOT the pathway.** I set out to confirm it and\ncould not:\n\n- Gerrit's git data is on a **separate EBS device**: `nvme1n1` carries\n  `/var/lib/docker/volumes/gerrit_git/_data` and `/var/gerrit`. `gate-scratch` is on\n  `nvme0n1p1` (root). Gerrit's packfile reads never touch the contended filesystem.\n- `iowait` was 17-18 % then fell to ~1 % while fetches remained slow. The dominant off-CPU\n  component tracks **CPU-credit exhaustion and runqueue depth**, not disk.\n\nThis is a **CPU/credit** capacity finding, not an IO one. `renowned-corked-hapuku` and\n`ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` remain real, but they are **not** the mechanism behind these 504s.\nThe three-ticket unification was attractive and is wrong.\n\n## Also refuted\n\n- **Jetty thread-pool exhaustion** (`httpd.maxThreads = 8`): refuted — every request is serviced\n  and returns 200, and the *slowest* requests ran at concurrency **0-1**, while requests at\n  concurrency 4-7 completed in 16-24 s. Concurrency is not the driver.\n- **JVM GC pause**: refuted — requests complete normally with a uniform ~24 % CPU/wall ratio\n  across 37 samples; a GC stall would not produce that, nor 100 % success.\n- **Slow ref advertisement**: refuted — `GET .../info/refs` completes in 4-31 ms in every sample.\n\n## AC3 — the distinction the ticket asked for\n\n**Both branches are live, and they are not equal.**\n\n1. **The proxy timeout is genuinely too short for legitimate packfile work.** Gerrit completes\n   100 % of these requests successfully. Raising it is a **fix, not a workaround** — it stops the\n   edge inventing a failure out of a success. This is the primary defect.\n2. **The host also cannot serve in reasonable time**, and that is a separate **capacity** finding\n   with a different remedy (credits/instance-class/tenancy), quantified above. Fixing only #2\n   still leaves a 60 s ceiling under a distribution that will re-cross it as the repo grows.\n\n## Defect origin (attribution)\n\nThe omission dates from **`6cd6f4e1f7c0`** (\"S2 (786b): deploy Gerrit + review-bot receiver behind\nnginx/TLS\", 2026-06-29) — ticket **`786b-190d-5e3a-4194` / `smelly-pane-bolt`**. That commit\ncreated the Gerrit `location /` with `proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;` and no timeout, and it has\nnever had one.\n\nThis is an **omission / scope-of-work attribution**, not a regression: no commit made the behaviour\nwrong. It became wrong when clone wall-time crossed 60 s under host capacity pressure, so the\nappropriate close class is **`preexisting`**, not `regression`.\n\n**The `caused_by` link is NOT recorded.** `rebar link ... caused_by` refuses the target\n(`786b` has no commit carrying its trailer) and offers only `--force`. Agents are prohibited from\nforce overrides, so this is escalated rather than bypassed: an operator should either record\n`caused_by` with a scope-of-work reason, or accept the attribution as documented here.\n\n## What I did NOT establish\n\n- I did not measure the *split* between the negotiation phase and `preparePack` inside the silent\n  window, so \"which sub-phase blows the 60 s\" is inferred from the header-phase signature, not\n  directly instrumented.\n- I did not establish that `rebar-mcp`'s 4.45 GB RSS is a leak rather than steady-state, nor that\n  it is the *cause* of credit exhaustion (it is the largest single consumer; that is correlation).\n- I did not test the fix. No timeout value has been validated against a real workload.\n- I did not establish the autodeploy trigger claimed in `1e24-bfc0`; my incidents do not need it.\n  Autodeploy is plausibly one of several things that push an already-credit-starved box over the\n  line, but it is not necessary to explain these four failures."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788648085610426001,"uuid":"6c22441a-16b4-454e-9aed-f47e01b254c8"}